{
  "dom_id": "step-5019",
  "name": "The Absolute Value of a Square",
  "problem": "We know that the absolute value operation does nothing to a number if it is non-negative. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|0| & =0 \\\\ |1| & =1 \\\\ |2| & =2 \\\\ |3| & =3 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this fact as follows:\n\n$$\n|x| = x \\quad \\text{ if } \\quad x \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe also know that $x^2$ is non-negative for any value of $x$. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\left|x^2\\right| = x^2\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is *any* real number.",
